.TH PR 1
.SH NAME
pr \- print files in pages and columns, with headings, in Limbo
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B pr
.B {+page}[bdefhilmnoprstw...]
[
.I file ...
]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Pr
is a structural Limbo translation of Plan 9's real
.IR pr (1)
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/pr.c ,
666 lines in the 9front tree), not a behavioral workalike \(em see
.B doc/plan9-port.md
in the source tree for the distinction, and
.B pr.b 's
own header comment for the fuller derivation this page summarises. It
lives under
.B p9/cmd
rather than
.BR appl/cmd ;
see
.B p9/dis/README
for how to bind it in.
.PP
.I Pr
paginates one or more files (or standard input) with a page
header giving the file's modification date, name, and page number,
and can lay the output out in multiple side-by-side columns or merge
several files column by column.
.PP
Verified against a real, live oracle: plan9port's own
.B pr
binary, run through this emu's host-command-execution device
.RB ( #C ,
see
.IR cmd (3))
on identical input, byte-diffed against this translation's own
output \(em see
.IR prtest (1)'s
own header comment for how, and why this file (previously left
blocked with "no checkable oracle exists") could be tackled at all.
.SS Options
.TP
.BI + n
Begin output at page
.IR n .
.TP
.B \-n
Number each line of output (see BUGS for a real side effect this has).
.TP
.BI \- N
A bare column count, e.g.
.BR \-3 .
Print in
.I N
columns.
.TP
.B \-a
Merge/mate the named files, numbering every merged line individually.
.TP
.BI \-m " file2 ..."
Merge/mate the named files, one line from each per output line. See
.IR pr.b 's
own header comment on how oddly this mode reaches its own printing
logic at all.
.TP
.B \-b
Balance the last, partial page's columns evenly rather than leaving
it ragged.
.TP
.B \-d
Double-space output.
.TP
.BI \-e "\fR[\fPc\fR]\fP[\fPn\fP]"
Expand input tabs (optionally set with character
.I c
and/or width
.IR n ,
default 8).
.TP
.B \-f
Use a formfeed between pages instead of blank lines.
.TP
.BI \-h " text"
Use
.I text
as the page header instead of the file name.
.TP
.BI \-i "\fR[\fPc\fR]\fP[\fPn\fP]"
Replace runs of output blanks with tabs (optionally set with character
.I c
and/or width
.IR n ,
default 8).
.TP
.BI \-l " n"
Set the page length to
.I n
lines (default 66).
.TP
.BI \-o " n"
Offset every line by
.I n
extra columns.
.TP
.B \-p
Pad with a blank (or formfeed, with
.BR -f )
page after an odd-numbered last page.
.TP
.BI \-s "\fR[\fPc\fR]\fP"
Separate columns with character
.I c
(default tab) instead of spaces.
.TP
.B \-t
Suppress the page header/footer entirely.
.TP
.BI \-w " n"
Set the line width to
.I n
(default 72 for multi-column output, 512 otherwise).
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/pr.b
(translated from
.BR sys/src/cmd/pr.c )
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR prtest (1)
.SH BUGS
A real bug in the source, found by reading it and CONFIRMED against
the oracle: option parsing's
.B \-n
case falls through into the
.B \-b
case with no
.B continue
in between (every other option ends with one) \(em so
.B \-n
unconditionally also enables last-page balancing, as an apparent
copy-paste/missing-continue mistake. Confirmed empirically: on a
20-line file laid out in 3 columns with a short page length,
.B "pr \-3"
leaves its last page ragged, but
.B "pr \-n \-3"
on the same file balances that same last page, with no
.B \-b
given at all. Reproduced explicitly here, not silently "fixed" \(em
see
.IR file (1)'s
own
.B dict[]
bug and
.IR tar (1)'s
own uid/gid bug for the precedent this tree follows on found-not-
fixed defects.
.PP
A second, vestigial finding, found by reading, not by testing: the
original's own deferred-error-message mechanism (a linked list its own
header comment describes, meant to defer a "can't open" report so it
doesn't disturb page alignment) is never actually populated anywhere
in the source \(em grepped for every place that would append to it,
found none. The real failure path prints immediately at the point of
failure instead; translated as what it actually does, not as the
deferred mechanism the stale comment describes.
.PP
The page header's date/time comes from Inferno's own
.B Daytime
module rather than reproducing the original's fixed-offset slice of a
.BR ctime ()-style
buffer (see
.B pr.b 's
own header comment for why that specific approach was rejected).
Its accuracy depends on this emu's own
.B /locale/timezone
configuration matching the host's real time zone \(em a real,
separate environmental fact, not a bug in this translation: if the two
disagree, the displayed hour will be off by the zone difference, same
as it would be for any other Inferno program relying on
.BR daytime (2).
.PP
Only the whole-file translation choices are novel here; the merge
.RB ( \-m / \-a )
mode's own real trigger \(em an already-open file set being handed to
the shared print routine via the same "no files named" fallback path
every other mode uses for reading standard input \(em is preserved
exactly as found, including the original's real all-or-nothing
behaviour: if even one file in a merge set fails to open, printing is
suppressed for the whole operation, not just the one bad file.
